POWER BI :- Concept Application
=============================================

Q1. Compare Import vs. DirectQuery modes and identify which is suitable for a static CSV file.
A1. Import mode loads data into Power BI's in-memory cache for fast performance and full DAX support, 
while DirectQuery queries the source in real-time for current data but slower performance. For static CSV files, 
use Import mode since data is static, file sizes are small, and offline capability is useful.

Q2. Explain the consequence of a "Text" data type on a Card visual displaying sales and identify where in the workflow to correct this.
A2. Text data type prevents aggregation functions (SUM, AVG) from working properly on sales values, causing incorrect or concatenated results on the Card visual. 
Correct this in Power Query Editor by clicking Transform Data, selecting the column, and changing the Data Type dropdown from Text to Whole Number or Currency.

Q3. Identify the specific menu or pane used to change a bar chart's sort order from alphabetical to descending by value.
A3. Click the bar chart, then in the Visualizations pane (right side), click the Sort axis button (three horizontal bars icon) in the toolbar. 
Click the dropdown next to it, select "Sort by Y-axis," and choose Descending. Alternatively, use Format pane > X-axis > Sort order.

Q4. Contrast filtering in Power Query versus using a visual-level "Top N" filter regarding their impact on other report visuals.
A4. Power Query filters remove data before it enters the model, affecting all visuals globally no other visual can see filtered rows. 
Visual-level "Top N" filters only affect that specific visual; other visuals continue to display the complete unfiltered dataset from the model.

Q5. Describe how to modify a bar chart or use a DAX measure to show a region's percentage of total sales instead of absolute values.
A5. Create a DAX measure: % of Total = DIVIDE(SUM([Sales]), CALCULATE(SUM([Sales]), ALL('Region'))). Add this measure to the chart's Values area and format as percentage in the Format pane. 
This shows each region's portion of total sales instead of absolute values.

Q6. Explain why hardcoded file paths cause shared PBIX files to break and how to make a report portable for other users.
A6. Hardcoded paths like "C:\Users\John\Desktop\file.xlsx" only exist on that user's machine other users have different usernames and folder structures, 
causing "file not found" errors. Make reports portable by using relative paths (e.g., ".\Data\file.xlsx"), storing data in SharePoint, 
or using environment variables so all users access files from the same location.
